      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Free radical UV-cure adhesives are based on acrylate and methacrylate monomers and oligomers. Exposure to UV light causes photoinitiator cleavage into free radicals that trigger chain-growth polymerisation of the acrylate double bonds — cure is very fast (fractions of a second under high-intensity sources) but is inhibited by atmospheric oxygen at the surface, leaving a tacky surface layer on open-face applications, and does not occur in shadow areas not reached by light. Cationic UV-cure systems use epoxide or vinyl ether monomers activated by diaryliodonium or triarylsulfonium salt photoinitiators that generate a strong Brønsted acid on UV exposure. The acid catalyst persists after the light source is removed, enabling dark cure — polymerisation continues in shadow areas after initial UV exposure — and the system is insensitive to oxygen inhibition. The trade-off is slower cure speed and sensitivity to basic contaminants (amines, moisture at high levels) that quench the cationic catalyst." }
